# torture.oracle-ext — cases where the gcc REFEREE runs a semantics OUTSIDE strict C99
# (the test hardcodes a gcc-specific value: either gcc-ext reduced-precision, or the test
# code itself contains UB and then demands a gcc fold). zcc follows the standard CORRECTLY → the differential zcc↔gcc reports
# a "mismatch" but the fault is in the ORACLE, not zcc. Each line: <case> <proof>. The PROOF must be
# MULTI-ANGLE (presumption-of-guilt law): an independent oracle (clang, the charter referee on macOS)
# plus the relevant C99 clause. A reviewer reproduces: `clang -std=c99 -w -O0 X.c && ./a.out; echo $?`.
# NOT a dumping ground for hidden bugs — only cases PROVEN to make gcc the outlier are admitted.
#
# reduced-precision arithmetic on a long-long bit-field (>32 bit): GCC performs the arithmetic at
# the field-width (pr32244 `x.b<<32`→40-bit→0); C99 6.3.1.1 only promotes a bit-field of
# _Bool/int/unsigned → a long-long field KEEPS its declared type → 64-bit arithmetic. clang -std=c99
# and zcc BOTH abort (64-bit, exit 134); only gcc returns 0. gcc is the outlier, zcc conforming.
pr32244-1  reduced-precision long-long-bitfield <<: clang+zcc abort (C99 6.3.1.1, 64-bit), gcc 40-bit outlier
pr34971    reduced-precision long-long-bitfield: clang+zcc abort (C99 6.3.1.1, 64-bit), gcc outlier
bitfld-3   reduced-precision long-long-bitfield *,+,-: clang+zcc abort (C99 6.3.1.1, 64-bit), gcc outlier
#
# UB — the program REDEFINES an identifier reserved by the standard library (C99
# 7.1.3: an external-linkage standard-library function name is always reserved; redefining it = UB).
# gcc+clang treat that name as a builtin and FOLD it (ignoring the user definition); zcc calls the real
# user function. A diff at UB is meaningless (charter). The test reveals its own intent: it defines llabs
# to call abort() and then expects it NOT to be called (i.e. it demands a fold).
20021127-1  UB redefine reserved `llabs` (C99 7.1.3); gcc+clang fold builtin dropping the user definition, zcc calls the real one → abort
